From 9a931fd51b3c798adc778e7008d07fd8b0ddebfb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:04:10 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 27/33] super-amd: create containers and volumes

- --create of a container: its disks are initialized as the BIOS menu
  does - both ends zeroed, an MBR with AMD's one partition, the label
  with the DeviceID RAIDCoreDxe gives the disk (from its serial number),
  a configuration listing them.  All RAID disks of a system share one
  configuration: a container is not created while another AMD RAID disk
  is there.
- --create of RAID0, RAID1, RAID10, RAID5 and Volume (linear) volumes in
  it, as the menu writes them, so that the BIOS and Windows take them;
  a redundant one gets the initialization md then runs (mdmon records
  it), or is recorded as initialized with --assume-clean.  Written by
  mdmon when it runs the container, by mdadm otherwise.
- --create -l1 -n1 --force makes a RAIDABLE, as the menu does: one
  whole disk no volume is on, no --size, laid out and recorded as the
  menu's Volume of that disk but for the type; md runs it as raid1 of
  one member.  The --force that -n1 needs does not go past the limit
  of 12 arrays.
- Of two --create run at once, the one that read the configuration
  before the other wrote it is refused and its md array stopped; a name
  another volume of the container has is refused, as by IMSM.
- A new volume takes the AMD global hot spares of the container too, as
  IMSM builds volumes of the spares of its container (a disk added to a
  container becomes one); they are spares no more, as the members of a
  volume the menu makes of Ready disks.  The AMD core would take them
  as they are (RC_CreateRaidArray does not look at SpareInfo).  A spare
  md is rebuilding onto, or one of a volume the monitor has not recorded
  yet, is not taken.
- --zero-superblock wipes the label, the commit block, the ring, and
  LBA 0 if it still holds the MBR AMD wrote.

The levels, numbers of disks and chunk sizes are those AMD allows.  A
volume is made on all its disks: "missing" is refused, as by IMSM, and
so are --write-zeroes and --data-offset.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
